== orlando_flights ==

Flights to Orlando

Orlando is one of the most-flown destinations in the country, which works in your favor. Competition keeps base fares reasonable. The catch is summer, holiday weeks, and spring break, when fares can double or triple. Book outside those windows and you can usually find a direct from a major U.S. city for $200 to $400 round trip per person.

When to book: 2 to 3 months out for domestic, 4 to 6 months for international, longer for peak weeks. Christmas, New Year's, spring break, and the week of July 4 fill up early and prices only go up. If you're flexible on dates, fly on Tuesdays or Wednesdays. Friday and Sunday flights are the most expensive of the week.

Direct vs connecting: Always pay extra for the direct flight if you're traveling with kids. The math on a $75 savings disappears the moment you're sprinting through the Atlanta airport with a stroller and a missed connection.

Carry-on strategy: Park trips destroy bags. Ponchos, sunscreen, snacks, portable chargers, and a change of clothes for the kids should all be in your carry-on, not your checked bag. If your checked bag goes to Denver instead of Orlando, you can still hit the parks tomorrow morning.

Pick the airport that gets you there at a reasonable hour. A 6 a.m. arrival means you can be in the park by lunch. An 11 p.m. arrival means you've burned a full day and you're starting tomorrow tired.

