== orlando_rental_cars ==

Rental Cars

Whether you need a rental car depends entirely on where you're staying and which resort you're visiting.

Staying on Disney property and only doing Disney: You don't need a car. Disney transportation (buses, monorail, Skyliner, boats) is free, runs constantly, and gets you everywhere on property. The exception is if you want to leave property for off-site dining, grocery runs, or a Universal day, in which case rideshare for those one-off trips usually costs less than a week of rental car plus parking.

Staying on Universal property: A car is recommended but not strictly required. Universal's three parks, CityWalk, and the on-site hotels are walkable or shuttle-accessible from each other, but Epic Universe is a separate campus a few miles south. The shuttle works. A car is faster.

Staying off property anywhere in Orlando: You need a car. The off-property hotel zones (International Drive, Lake Buena Vista, Kissimmee, Celebration) all assume you're driving to the parks. Rideshare twice a day across a week-long trip costs more than a rental.

Doing both Disney and Universal: Get a car. The transportation between resorts is not built for daily back-and-forth.

MCO has the rental car center inside the terminal. Walk off your flight, follow signs, you're at the counter in 10 minutes. Sanford rental car pickup is also on-site but the airport is smaller. Book in advance, especially for summer and holiday weeks. Walk-up rates at MCO during peak season are punishing.

Toll roads: Orlando uses electronic tolls. Almost every rental car company offers a transponder add-on, usually a few dollars per day plus the actual tolls. Worth it. Driving past toll cameras without a transponder generates plate-billed tolls with hefty admin fees.

